{% call section("Research", "research") %}
<p>I pursue three research programs. The first is on the foundations of AI safety. How should an artificial agent represent the world, value outcomes, and act? With <a href="https://www.cmu.edu/dietrich/philosophy/people/faculty/london.html">Alex London</a>, I extend decision theory so that an agent can refine its values as well as its beliefs, and prove a value-of-information theorem for learning about values. With <a href="https://www.danielherrmann.ca/">Daniel Herrmann</a>, Ben Levinstein, and Bruce Rushing, I show that causal reasoning reduces to subjective probability once interventions are treated as events in the world. I also study when we should delegate to AI systems whose aims may diverge from ours, and how to allocate compute when AI agents do safety research themselves. With Daniel, I co-direct <a href="https://exproof.ai/"><span class="exists" aria-hidden="true">&exist;</span><span class="visually-hidden">E</span>xProof AI</a>.</p>
<p>The second is metascience: how scientific communities produce knowledge, and how to do it better. What a community learns depends on how individual inquiry is aggregated, connected, incentivized, and funded, and these properties often cannot be read off individual reasoning. A reform can improve every study and still worsen the published literature. I study grant funding, replication and reform, and how networks of scientists learn from one another.</p>
<p>The third is on method. I use Bayesian epistemology, decision theory, and evolutionary game theory, and I study when these tools mislead: when different models of evolution disagree about what evolves, and why, and when equilibrium concepts miss the outcomes that matter.</p>
{% endcall %}

{% call section("Teaching and mentoring", "teaching") %}
<p>I teach decision theory, game theory, AI safety, logic, and philosophy of science at Carnegie Mellon; the full list is on the <a href="{{ '/teaching/' | url }}">Teaching</a> page. I mentor AI safety researchers through <a href="https://sparai.org/">SPAR</a> and advise master&rsquo;s students.</p>
{% endcall %}

{% call section("Public writing", "writing") %}
<p>I write philosophy posts on <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/users/aydin-mohseni">LessWrong</a>, among them <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/auSfqhbMKEvzt4unG/chance-is-in-the-map-not-the-territory">Chance is in the Map, not the Territory</a> and <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/qQTXjpXbcXMHvExmf/evolution-and-the-low-road-to-nash">Evolution and the Low Road to Nash</a>.</p>
{% endcall %}

